  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1145 PM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nOur 00Z RAOB measured a PWAT of 1.70\". This is well over the\n90th percentile of 1.15\". The max for this day per the SPC RAOB\nclimo was 1.56\". The bottom line is that we are dealing with an\nincredibly wet atmosphere for this time of year. At the mid\nlevel of the atmosphere, satellite water vapor channel shows a\nmid level trof working across the southern Rockies. Ahead of\nthis disturbance, radar is showing plenty of showers across the\nentire region. As a result, the scattered soaking rain showers \nwill continue to work across the FA through the early morning \nhours. Several CAMs continue to indicate various rainfall \namounts falling this morning and later today. There seems to be \na general consensus of 0.5\" to 2.0\". Some of CAMs are\nforecasting 3+\" through early Friday morning. The latest \nofficial NBM/WPC grid has rainfall amounts in the 0.5\" to 2.0\",\nwith isolated higher amounts. There is a little bit of \nuncertainty in exactly how much additional showers will develop\nlater this afternoon. Given the very moist atmosphere, it seems\nprudent to continue to hold on to higher pops later today given\nthe very moist atmosphere. I did ask neighbors (PUB and GLD) if\nthey wanted to join AMA in issuing any flood headlines. The \nduration of this event is rather long and it has been very dry \nrecently, so a consensus of no headlines was reached on this \nshift. Otherwise, enjoy the cloudy, rainy and cool day. We \ncertainly needed this type of weather day given the above normal\nheat we have been experiencing.\n\nLooking ahead, addition widely scattered showers and perhaps a\nrumble of thunder will be possible for Friday. Areal coverage\nshould start to be lesser Friday than compared to Thursday, as \nPWATs are forecast to decrease through the day and as the main \nsynoptic trof starts to swing out of the general region. By \nSaturday and Sunday, GEFS and ENS are forecasting shortwave \nridging with drier air continue to advect in from the west and \nnorthwest. This would support the notion of a drier and warmer \nforecast. Highs over the weekend will likely trend to be above \nnormal with values peaking solidly in the low to mid 80s. Lows \nwill also trend to be above normal with values only bottoming \nout mainly in the 60s than compared to the normal 50s.\n\nFor next week, long range models and their ensemble counterparts\nare forecasting another trof starting to carve out across the\nsouthwestern United States. Moisture advection looks to increase\nonce again downstream of this mid level disturbance with PWATs\nreaching higher values across the Great Plains. This moisture\nalong with some mid level waves moving across should set the \nstage of showers and isolated thunderstorms as we start to head \nthrough the beginning to mid period of the next business \nweek.",
